  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 207 PM MDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nSaturday into Sunday...The southern low from the aforementioned \nsplit will begin to deepen over AZ and help moisture return to CO. \nThis moisture along with WAA and good synoptic forcing will support \nmore widespread precipitation across the region. Light to moderate \nrain is expected, but a locally heavy shower cannot be ruled out. \nWPC's Day 3 (Sat) Excessive Rain Outlook includes parts of SE CO in \nthe SLGT risk area and was updated to include western CO in a MRGL \nrisk earlier today. Day 4 (Sun) also has part of the region in a \nSLGT risk, but a much smaller area in far SE CO. There is the \npotential for flash flooding in areas which experience locally \nhigher rainfall totals for both Saturday and Sunday. QPFs of 1.5-2 \ninches are expected across SE CO over the weekend, with locally \nhigher totals possible. Given this system's pattern of producing \nsevere weather across the Desert Southwest, it is not out of the \nrealm of possibility for a low end severe threat to exist on \nSaturday. This will depend on the position of the instability axis, \nits strength, and its northern extent. The northern low will help \npush a cold front into CO on Saturday faster and further south than \nprevious guidance showed. High temperatures will drop into the 50s \nand 60s across the high valleys and plains. With colder \ntemperatures, snow levels will drop to 9,500ft. It is possible \nthat a few inches of snow could accumulate on terrain above \n10,000ft. These colder temps will persist through the weekend and \ninto Monday.\n\nMonday through Wednesday...Residual isolated showers are possible \nMonday from the outgoing weekend system. Late Monday, the low will \nmove out of CO into NE, taking the moisture in the area with it. \nBroad upper level ridging will develop over the region for Tuesday \nand Wednesday. Gradual warming begins Tuesday, with generally dry \nand more seasonably type temperatures in place by midweek.",
